gRPC, which Google open-sourced in 2015, is RPC again, but with the network kept in view. You describe the service in a `.proto` file, and generators turn it into a client stub and a server base class for each language:

```text
package payments.v1;

service Charges {
  rpc Charge(ChargeRequest) returns (ChargeReply);
  rpc WatchBatch(BatchRef) returns (stream BatchProgress);
}

message ChargeRequest {
  string payment_id = 1;
  int64 amount_cents = 2;
  string idempotency_key = 3;
}
```

The messages are Protobuf, so they evolve by the rules of lesson 2 of this track: new fields get new numbers, and old readers skip what they don't know. Each call is one HTTP/2 stream (the Networking track's lesson on HTTP/2): a POST to the path `/payments.v1.Charges/Charge`, then each message as one flag byte, a four-byte length and the Protobuf bytes. The outcome travels at the end, as `grpc-status` in the trailers (headers sent after the last message). Since a stream can carry many messages each way, there are four kinds of method: unary (one request, one reply, like `Charge`), server streaming (`WatchBatch` pushes progress until the settlement batch is done), client streaming and bidirectional streaming.

For payment code, the part that matters most is the **deadline**. gRPC sets no deadline unless you do, so a call whose reply is lost can wait forever while it holds a thread. The client sets one per call, for example `withDeadlineAfter(300, MILLISECONDS)`. It travels in a `grpc-timeout` header as time remaining, not as a clock time, so the two machines' clocks don't need to agree. The server sees how much time is left, and grpc-java and Go pass that remainder on to any call the server makes while it handles the request. When the time runs out, the client gets `DEADLINE_EXCEEDED` and the server's side of the call is cancelled. The Try below sends one deadline through two services.

A call ends with one of 17 status codes, `OK` among them. For retries, four of the others matter. `UNAVAILABLE` usually means the request or reply didn't get through, and the gRPC docs call it most likely transient. `DEADLINE_EXCEEDED` means nobody knows. `INVALID_ARGUMENT` and `FAILED_PRECONDITION` come back the same however often you ask. gRPC can also retry by itself, with a retry policy in the channel's service config; the worked example sets one up.

Browsers can't make raw gRPC calls, because their HTTP APIs don't expose HTTP/2 frames and trailers. gRPC-Web and Connect fill the gap. Connect, from Buf, keeps the `.proto` contract; its servers answer gRPC, gRPC-Web and the Connect protocol, which also works over HTTP/1.1 and can be called with `curl` and JSON. Its Go, TypeScript and Swift libraries are stable; Kotlin is still beta.

